SUMMARY:Memory Transformed Symposium: Materials of Heritage in Jewish Eas
 tern European Life
DESCRIPTION:Join the Borns Jewish Studies Program for the opening symposi
 um of Memory Transformed: Materials of Heritage in Jewish Eastern Europea
 n Life\, a day of scholarship\, art\, and music exploring how Eastern Eur
 opean Jewish culture is remembered and continues to shape contemporary ar
 tistic production.11:00 a.m. — Complimentary Brunch at the Tudor Room*T
 he complimentary brunch at the Tudor Room is currently at capacity. If sp
 ace becomes available\, registered attendees will be contacted.*12:30–3
 :00 p.m. — SymposiumDogwood Room\, Indiana Memorial Union  The symposiu
 m will feature two panels examining Jewish material culture\, music\, mem
 ory\, and the cultural afterlives of objects associated with Eastern Euro
 pean Jewish life. Scholars and artists will explore how recordings\, phot
 ographs\, books\, memorials\, and other cultural objects preserve knowled
 ge\, shape memory\, and inspire contemporary cultural production.Panel 1J
 udith BerksonPatterns of Improvisation in Live Cantorial Recordings\, 195
 0–1980Jeremiah LockwoodPlastinkes and idisher rekords: Towards a Histor
 y of Jewish Sacred Pop Music as Material CultureDov-Ber KerlerFrom the Ne
 w to the Old World and Vice Versa: Contrafacts\, Adaptation and Radical R
 eworkingPanel 2Halina GoldbergMaterial Culture\, Memory\, and the Making 
 of a Museum and Digital Project on Jewish ŁódźNicolette van den Bogerd
 Composing Jewish Musical Material Culture After the HolocaustJennifer McC
 omasTransformations of Material and Memory in Ruth Weinberg's Shtetl Port
 folioModerators  Joanna Niżyńska  Sergio Ospina-Romero  The symposium i
 s part of the larger Memory Transformed program taking place throughout t
 he day on Sunday\, October 11\, including brunch\, an afternoon reception
  and optional guided museum tours at the Eskenazi Museum of Art\, and an 
 evening concert at Auer Hall.Free and open to the public. Registration is
  encouraged.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  Join the Borns Jewish Studies Program f
 or the opening symposium of Memory Transformed: Materials of Heritage in 
 Jewish Eastern European Life\, a day of scholarship\, art\, and music exp
 loring how Eastern European Jewish culture is remembered and continues to
  shape contemporary artistic production.</p><p><strong>11:00 a.m. —</st
 rong> Complimentary Brunch at the Tudor Room</p><p><strong>*The complimen
 tary brunch at the Tudor Room is currently at capacity. If space becomes 
 available\, registered attendees will be contacted.*</strong></p><p><stro
 ng>12:30–3:00 p.m. — Symposium</strong><br /><strong>Dogwood Room\, I
 ndiana Memorial Union</strong></p><p>  The symposium will feature two pan
 els examining Jewish material culture\, music\, memory\, and the cultural
  afterlives of objects associated with Eastern European Jewish life. Scho
 lars and artists will explore how recordings\, photographs\, books\, memo
 rials\, and other cultural objects preserve knowledge\, shape memory\, an
 d inspire contemporary cultural production.</p><p><strong>Panel 1</strong
 ></p><p><strong>Judith Berkson</strong><br /><em>Patterns of Improvisatio
 n in Live Cantorial Recordings\, 1950–1980</em></p><p><strong>Jeremiah 
 Lockwood</strong><br /><em>Plastinkes and idisher rekords: Towards a Hist
 ory of Jewish Sacred Pop Music as Material Culture</em></p><p><strong>Dov
 -Ber Kerler</strong><br /><em>From the New to the Old World and Vice Vers
 a: Contrafacts\, Adaptation and Radical Reworking</em></p><p><strong>Pane
 l 2</strong></p><p><strong>Halina Goldberg</strong><br /><em>Material Cul
 ture\, Memory\, and the Making of a Museum and Digital Project on Jewish 
 Łódź</em></p><p><strong>Nicolette van den Bogerd</strong><br /><em>Com
 posing Jewish Musical Material Culture After the Holocaust</em></p><p><st
 rong>Jennifer McComas</strong><br /><em>Transformations of Material and M
 emory in Ruth Weinberg's Shtetl Portfolio</em></p><p><strong>Moderators</
 strong><br />  Joanna Niżyńska<br />  Sergio Ospina-Romero</p><p>  The 
 symposium is part of the larger Memory Transformed program taking place t
 hroughout the day on Sunday\, October 11\, including brunch\, an afternoo
 n reception and optional guided museum tours at the Eskenazi Museum of Ar
 t\, and an evening concert at Auer Hall.</p><p><strong>Free and open to t
 he public. Registration is encouraged.</strong></p>
