SUMMARY:Fall 2026 CLACS Faculty Research Symposium with Luis A. González
DESCRIPTION:CLACS Faculty Research Symposium welcomes Luis A. González\,
  Librarian for Latin American\, Spanish &amp\; Portuguese\, Latino\, and 
 European Studies\, and an Adjunct faculty in History at Indiana Universit
 y as he presents:&#160\;Books in Formerly Sacred Spaces: The Story of a P
 ublic Library\, a President\, and an Artist — Jiquilpan\, Mexico\, 1941
   &#160\;Abstract:  This presentation considers the Gabino Ortiz Public L
 ibrary\, notably founded by President Lázaro Cárdenas in his hometown o
 f Jiquilpan\, Michoacán\, in 1941. As part of his national cultural proj
 ect\, President Cárdenas opened the library within a repurposed church w
 hich also houses murals by José Clemente Orozco depicting events from th
 e Mexican Revolution. This case study presents a compelling opportunity t
 o examine the intersections of state cultural policy\, collective memory\
 , and the social imaginary of the library in modern Mexico.  &#160\;Biogr
 aphy:  Luis A. González is the Librarian for Latin American\, Spanish &a
 mp\; Portuguese\, Latino\, and European Studies\, and an Adjunct faculty 
 in History at Indiana University. He has published in the fields of Brazi
 lian agrarian\, social\, and socio-legal history\, and in Latin American 
 Studies librarianship. Luis is a Contributing Editor for the Handbook of 
 Latin American Studies\, published by the Hispanic Division of the Librar
 y of Congress\, and past president of the Seminar on the Acquisition of L
 atin American Library Materials (SALALM).&#160\;  &#160\;
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  CLACS Faculty Research Symposium welcom
 es <strong>Luis A. González\,</strong> Librarian for Latin American\, Sp
