 faculty in History at Indiana University as he presents:&#160\;<br /><br 
 /></p><p><em><strong>Books in Formerly Sacred Spaces: The Story of a Publ
 ic Library\, a President\, and an Artist — Jiquilpan\, Mexico\, 1941</s
 trong></em></p><p>  &#160\;</p><p><strong>Abstract:</strong></p><p>  This
  presentation considers the Gabino Ortiz Public Library\, notably founded
  by President Lázaro Cárdenas in his hometown of Jiquilpan\, Michoacán
 \, in 1941. As part of his national cultural project\, President Cárdena
 s opened the library within a repurposed church which also houses murals 
 by José Clemente Orozco depicting events from the Mexican Revolution. Th
 is case study presents a compelling opportunity to examine the intersecti
 ons of state cultural policy\, collective memory\, and the social imagina
 ry of the library in modern Mexico.</p><p>  &#160\;</p><p><strong>Biograp
 hy:</strong></p><p>  Luis A. González is the Librarian for Latin America
