BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:IU Hamilton Lugar School
DTSTART:20261013T220000Z
DTEND:20261013T230000Z
SUMMARY:Chinese Tidings: "The Belt and Road Initiative: Domestic Policyma
 king and Global Impacts"
DESCRIPTION:Pizza is served at 5:30 pm. and the lecture starts at 6:00 pm
 .About the lecture: Launched in 2013\, the Belt and Road Initiative (BRI)
  occupies a central position in China's contemporary foreign policy and d
 omestic development  agenda. While often described as an infrastructure i
 nvestment program\, the BRI encompasses multiple dimensions of developmen
 t policymaking\, trade and investment facilitation\, financial cooperatio
 n\, and people-to-people exchange. Understanding how the initiative is or
 ganized and implemented is crucial for making sense of China's evolving p
 osition in the global economic and political system. This lecture provide
 s a framework for understanding the BRI\, with particular attention to it
 s domestic organizing mechanisms\, how they shape China's engagement with
  partner countries\, and the broader implications of the initiative for g
 lobal development.About the presenter: Dr. Hong Zhang is an Assistant Pro
 fessor in the Department of International Studies at the Hamilton Lugar S
 chool. Her research focuses on the political economy of global developmen
 t and China's role in it. She teaches courses on global development\, inf
 rastructure\, and China–Global South relations. She is currently writin
 g a book on China's developmental statecraft\, examining how  China build
 s structural power in the global economy while strengthening its  develop
 mental state at home.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  Pizza is served at 5:30 pm. and the lec
 ture starts at 6:00 pm.</p><p><em>About the lecture:</em> Launched in 201
 3\, the Belt and Road Initiative (BRI) occupies a central position in Chi
 na's contemporary foreign policy and domestic development<br />  agenda. 
 While often described as an infrastructure investment program\, the BRI e
 ncompasses multiple dimensions of development policymaking\, trade and in
 vestment facilitation\, financial cooperation\, and people-to-people exch
 ange. Understanding how the initiative is organized and implemented is cr
 ucial for making sense of China's evolving position in the global economi
 c and political system. This lecture provides a framework for understandi
 ng the BRI\, with particular attention to its domestic organizing mechani
 sms\, how they shape China's engagement with partner countries\, and the 
 broader implications of the initiative for global development.<br /><span
  class="EOP SCXW157701014 BCX0" data-ccp-props="{&quot\;335551550&quot\;:
 1\,&quot\;335551620&quot\;:1}"></span></p><p><em>About the presenter: Dr.
  Hong Zhang is an Assistant Professor in the Department of International 
 Studies at the Hamilton Lugar School. Her research focuses on the politic
 al economy of global development and China's role in it. She teaches cour
 ses on global development\, infrastructure\, and China–Global South rel
 ations. She is currently writing a book on China's developmental statecra
 ft\, examining how<br />  China builds structural power in the global eco
 nomy while strengthening its<br />  developmental state at home.</em></p>
