LOCATION:The Ostrom Workshop\, 513 N PARK AVE
GEO:39.171057;-86.524959
SUMMARY:Ostrom Workshop | Research Series: Diego Pacheco
DESCRIPTION:Bio  Diego Pacheco Balanza is a Bolivian researcher\, public 
 policy specialist\, and former diplomat with over two decades of experien
 ce in sustainable development\, environmental governance\, and internatio
 nal negotiations. He holds a Ph.D. in Public Policy from Indiana Universi
 ty\, where he studied under Nobel Laureate Elinor Ostrom\, an MSc in Rura
 l Resources and Environmental Policy from Imperial College London\, and a
  BA in Anthropology.  His work has focused on the relationships between s
 ociety and Nature\, institutional governance\, Indigenous Peoples\, clima
 te change\, biodiversity\, and the development of the Living Well (Vivir 
 Bien) approach and cosmobiocentric perspectives for sustainability. Most 
 recently\, he served as Director General for Living Well and Foreign Poli
 cy at the Office of the Vice President of the Plurinational State of Boli
 via and as Chargé d'Affaires a.i. at the Permanent Mission of Bolivia to
  the United Nations in New York. He has also served twice as Bolivia's Vi
 ce Minister of Planning and represented the country in multilateral envir
 onmental negotiations under the UNFCCC\, CBD\, and IPBES. He is the autho
 r of numerous books and scientific publications on public policy\, Mother
  Earth\, natural resources governance and sustainable development. His cu
 rrent research explores new pathways for global governance through the in
 tegration of indigenous knowledge\, collective action\, and Mother Earth-
 centered approaches.Title  From Social–Ecological Systems toward Life S
 ystems: Expanding Ostromian Institutional Analysis toward Mother Earth Fu
 turesAbstract  Building on the growing recognition of Indigenous and loca
 l knowledge systems\, plural worldviews\, and relational approaches to hu
 man–Nature relationships\, this talk explores how the Ostrom Workshop t
 radition of institutional analysis and collective action theory might be 
 expanded to engage these non-anthropocentric perspectives. It considers a
  shift from an analytical focus on human actors as the primary agents of 
 collective action within social–ecological systems toward Communities o
 f Life within Life Systems\, and introduces an analytical framework and e
 mpirical architecture for examining Human–Mother Earth co-flourishing. 
 This also speaks to an emerging research gap concerning the joint examina
 tion of human and more-than-human outcomes\, rather than treating their r
 elationship predominantly in one direction.About the Ostrom Workshop Rese
 arch Series  Learn about innovative research by Graduate Students and Vis
 iting Scholars as they advance the mission of the Ostrom Workshop\, build
 ing upon the theme of governance to understand and address major societal
  problems.
