 p>  Diego Pacheco Balanza is a Bolivian researcher\, public policy specia
 list\, and former diplomat with over two decades of experience in sustain
 able development\, environmental governance\, and international negotiati
 ons. He holds a Ph.D. in Public Policy from Indiana University\, where he
  studied under Nobel Laureate Elinor Ostrom\, an MSc in Rural Resources a
 nd Environmental Policy from Imperial College London\, and a BA in Anthro
 pology.</p><p>  His work has focused on the relationships between society
  and Nature\, institutional governance\, Indigenous Peoples\, climate cha
 nge\, biodiversity\, and the development of the <em>Living Well (Vivir Bi
 en)</em> approach and cosmobiocentric perspectives for sustainability. Mo
 st recently\, he served as Director General for Living Well and Foreign P
 olicy at the Office of the Vice President of the Plurinational State of B
 olivia and as Chargé d'Affaires a.i. at the Permanent Mission of Bolivia
  to the United Nations in New York. He has also served twice as Bolivia's
  Vice Minister of Planning and represented the country in multilateral en
 vironmental negotiations under the UNFCCC\, CBD\, and IPBES. He is the au
 thor of numerous books and scientific publications on public policy\, Mot
 her Earth\, natural resources governance and sustainable development. His
  current research explores new pathways for global governance through the
  integration of indigenous knowledge\, collective action\, and Mother Ear
 th-centered approaches.</p><p><strong>Title</strong></p><p>  From Social�
 ��Ecological Systems toward Life Systems: Expanding Ostromian Institution
 al Analysis toward Mother Earth Futures</p><p><strong>Abstract</strong></
 p><p>  Building on the growing recognition of Indigenous and local knowle
 dge systems\, plural worldviews\, and relational approaches to human–Na
 ture relationships\, this talk explores how the Ostrom Workshop tradition
  of institutional analysis and collective action theory might be expanded
  to engage these non-anthropocentric perspectives. It considers a shift f
 rom an analytical focus on human actors as the primary agents of collecti
 ve action within social–ecological systems toward Communities of Life w
 ithin Life Systems\, and introduces an analytical framework and empirical
  architecture for examining Human–Mother Earth co-flourishing. This als
 o speaks to an emerging research gap concerning the joint examination of 
 human and more-than-human outcomes\, rather than treating their relations
 hip predominantly in one direction.</p><p><strong>About the Ostrom Worksh
 op Research Series</strong></p><p>  Learn about innovative research by Gr
 aduate Students and Visiting Scholars as they advance the mission of the 
 Ostrom Workshop\, building upon the theme of governance to understand and
  address major societal problems.</p>
