SUMMARY:Social Structures and Developments within the Early Zoroastrian C
 ommunity as Reflected in the Avesta
DESCRIPTION:The Avesta is a valuable historical source for reconstructing
  the social organization of the early Zoroastrian community. The sacred a
 nd ritual character of the text predetermined the use of stable formulas 
 and the creation of an ideal model of society. Nevertheless\, behind this
  standardized framework\, one can reconstruct mechanisms of intra‑commu
 nal regulation\, power\, and status relations.  This talk offers a system
 ic view of the social structure of the early Zoroastrian community\, draw
 ing on an analysis of the social terminology and the content of the Avest
 a. The presence of chronological layers within the Avesta allows us to tr
 ace the gradual increase in complexity of social institutions\, as well a
 s the evolution of interpersonal relations and power structures in the Zo
 roastrian community. These changes reflect the transition from small trib
 al collectives to more complex pre‑state formations. Thus\, the Avesta 
 recorded not so much a static model of society as the dynamics of develop
 ment and adaptation of its social institutions.This event will take place
  on Zoom only.This event is sponsored by:Department of Central Eurasian S
 tudies (CEUS)Inner Asian and Uralic National Resource Center (IAUNRC)
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  The Avesta is a valuable historical sou
 rce for reconstructing the social organization of the early Zoroastrian c
 ommunity. The sacred and ritual character of the text predetermined the u
 se of stable formulas and the creation of an ideal model of society. Neve
 rtheless\, behind this standardized framework\, one can reconstruct mecha
 nisms of intra‑communal regulation\, power\, and status relations.</p><
 p>  This talk offers a systemic view of the social structure of the early
  Zoroastrian community\, drawing on an analysis of the social terminology
  and the content of the Avesta. The presence of chronological layers with
 in the Avesta allows us to trace the gradual increase in complexity of so
 cial institutions\, as well as the evolution of interpersonal relations a
 nd power structures in the Zoroastrian community. These changes reflect t
 he transition from small tribal collectives to more complex pre‑state f
 ormations. Thus\, the Avesta recorded not so much a static model of socie
 ty as the dynamics of development and adaptation of its social institutio
 ns.</p><p><strong>This event will take place on Zoom only.</strong></p><p
 ><em>This event is sponsored by:</em></p><ul><li><em>Department of Centra
 l Eurasian Studies (CEUS)</em></li><li><em>Inner Asian and Uralic Nationa
 l Resource Center (IAUNRC)</em></li></ul>
