SUMMARY:From Auditory Impressions to Linguistic Analysis: Toward a Taxono
 my of Prosody
DESCRIPTION:A listener's casual impressions of a language's sound are sha
 ped by several prosodic properties\, including speech rate\, rhythm\, tim
 ing\, pitch\, and loudness. Although these features can readily be measur
 ed instrumentally\, mapping them to a linguistic analysis of a language's
  prosody is often a daunting task. For example\, it can be difficult to d
 etermine whether a rise or fall in pitch reflects phrasal intonation or w
 ord stress. Similarly\, it may be unclear whether increased duration at t
 he end of a word reflects linguistic stress or instead results from the c
 ross-linguistically common phenomenon of boundary-related lengthening. Gi
 ven these challenges\, it is unsurprising that analyses of the same langu
 age often differ substantially—for example\, Khalkha Mongolian and Kaza
 kh have been variously analyzed as languages with word stress or with phr
 asal intonation only.In this talk\, we explore the empirical basis for ev
 aluating different potential prosodic analyses of a particular language. 
 Through case studies of a number of languages of Central Asia and Eastern
  Europe\, we examine the criteria that have been used in the prosodic cla
 ssification of languages\, with an eye toward developing a more consisten
 t set of diagnostics for the linguistic analysis of prosody.This event wi
 ll take place both in person and on Zoom. Refreshments will be provided.T
 his event is sponsored by:Department of Central Eurasian Studies (CEUS)In
 ner Asian and Uralic National Resource Center (IAUNRC)
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><span data-olk-copy-source="MessageBody">
 A listener's casual impressions of a language's sound are shaped by sever
 al prosodic properties\, including speech rate\, rhythm\, timing\, pitch\
 , and loudness. Although these features can readily be measured instrumen
 tally\, mapping them to a linguistic analysis of a language's prosody is 
 often a daunting task. For example\, it can be difficult to determine whe
 ther a rise or fall in pitch reflects phrasal intonation or word stress. 
 Similarly\, it may be unclear whether increased duration at the end of a 
 word reflects linguistic stress or instead results from the cross-linguis
 tically common phenomenon of boundary-related lengthening. Given these ch
 allenges\, it is unsurprising that analyses of the same language often di
 ffer substantially—for example\, Khalkha Mongolian and Kazakh have been
  variously analyzed as languages with word stress or with phrasal intonat
 ion only.</span></p><p><span data-olk-copy-source="MessageBody">In this t
 alk\, we explore the empirical basis for evaluating different potential p
 rosodic analyses of a particular language. Through case studies of a numb
 er of languages of Central Asia and Eastern Europe\, we examine the crite
 ria that have been used in the prosodic classification of languages\, wit
 h an eye toward developing a more consistent set of diagnostics for the l
 inguistic analysis of prosody.</span></p><p><strong>This event will take 
 place both in person and on Zoom. Refreshments will be provided.</strong>
 </p><p><em>This event is sponsored by:</em></p><ul><li><em>Department of 
 Central Eurasian Studies (CEUS)</em></li><li><em>Inner Asian and Uralic N
 ational Resource Center (IAUNRC)</em></li></ul>
