SUMMARY:Global Competencies Skills Development Series: Mapping a Refugee 
 Camp — Interactive Policy Simulation with Ambassador Gina Abercrombie-W
 instanley
DESCRIPTION:Real Crisis. Real Decisions. Real Tradecraft.  When displacem
 ent crises hit\, how do international leaders manage logistics\, security
 \, human rights\, and geopolitical pressure in real time?  Skip the stand
 ard lecture and get your hands on actual diplomatic decision-making. Join
  the Global Competencies Skills Development Series for Mapping a Refugee 
 Camp\, an interactive\, fast-paced crisis response simulation led by Amba
 ssador Gina Abercrombie-Winstanley—former U.S. Ambassador to Malta\, 30
 -year Foreign Service veteran\, and President of the Middle East Policy C
 ouncil.  Having led emergency war-zone evacuations and served across Bagh
 dad\, Cairo\, and Jeddah\, Ambassador Abercrombie-Winstanley will guide y
 ou through the complex tradecraft\, resource constraints\, and tactical c
 hoices required on the front lines of global humanitarian crisis manageme
 nt.  Who Can Attend? Open to all: Students (Undergrad &amp\; Grad)\, Facu
 lty\, Staff\, and the Public. (Light snacks and drinks provided)  &#160\;
   What You'll Get Out of It      Hands-On Tactical Experience: Practice r
 eal-world decision-making under competing geopolitical\, logistical\, and
  ethical pressures.          Direct Professional Access: Work through cri
 sis scenarios directly alongside a senior diplomat who operated across th
 e State Department\, Defense Department\, and National Security Council. 
          Resume-Building Tradecraft: Build core skills in crisis navigati
 on\, strategic negotiation\, and rapid resource allocation—essential fo
 r careers in foreign policy\, intelligence\, NGOs\, and national security
 .          Active Networking: Team up with peers\, faculty\, and practiti
 oners across campus over light refreshments.      Bio: Ambassador Gina Ab
 ercrombie-Winstanley  Ambassador Gina Abercrombie-Winstanley is a 30-year
  diplomat\, former U.S. Ambassador to the Republic of Malta\, and Preside
 nt of the Middle East Policy Council. Her career spans executive leadersh
 ip roles across the Department of State\, Defense Department\, National S
 ecurity Council\, and Senate Foreign Relations Committee. She has advised
  the commander of U.S. cyber forces\, expanded international counterterro
 rism partnerships\, and led the emergency evacuation of thousands of U.S.
  citizens from a war zone. She is a recipient of the State Department Sup
 erior Honor Award for acts of courage during an al-Qaeda attack on the U.
 S. Consulate in Jeddah.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<h4 data-path-to-node="13">  Real Crisis. Re
 al Decisions. Real Tradecraft.</h4><p data-path-to-node="14">  When displ
 acement crises hit\, how do international leaders manage logistics\, secu
 rity\, human rights\, and geopolitical pressure in real time?</p><p data-
 path-to-node="15">  Skip the standard lecture and get your hands on actua
 l diplomatic decision-making. Join the Global Competencies Skills Develop
 ment Series for Mapping a Refugee Camp\, an interactive\, fast-paced cris
 is response simulation led by Ambassador Gina Abercrombie-Winstanley—fo
 rmer U.S. Ambassador to Malta\, 30-year Foreign Service veteran\, and Pre
 sident of the Middle East Policy Council.</p><p data-path-to-node="16">  
 Having led emergency war-zone evacuations and served across Baghdad\, Cai
 ro\, and Jeddah\, Ambassador Abercrombie-Winstanley will guide you throug
 h the complex tradecraft\, resource constraints\, and tactical choices re
 quired on the front lines of global humanitarian crisis management.</p><h
 4 data-path-to-node="17">  Who Can Attend? Open to all: Students (Undergr
 ad &amp\; Grad)\, Faculty\, Staff\, and the Public. <i data-path-to-node=
 "18" data-index-in-node="74">(Light snacks and drinks provided)</i></h4><
 h4 data-path-to-node="19">  &#160\;</h4><h4 data-path-to-node="19">  What
  You'll Get Out of It</h4><ul data-path-to-node="20"><li><p data-path-to-
 node="20\,0\,0">      Hands-On Tactical Experience: Practice real-world d
 ecision-making under competing geopolitical\, logistical\, and ethical pr
 essures.    </p></li><li><p data-path-to-node="20\,1\,0">      Direct Pro
 fessional Access: Work through crisis scenarios directly alongside a seni
 or diplomat who operated across the State Department\, Defense Department
 \, and National Security Council.    </p></li><li><p data-path-to-node="2
 0\,2\,0">      Resume-Building Tradecraft: Build core skills in crisis na
 vigation\, strategic negotiation\, and rapid resource allocation—essent
 ial for careers in foreign policy\, intelligence\, NGOs\, and national se
 curity.    </p></li><li><p data-path-to-node="20\,3\,0">      Active Netw
 orking: Team up with peers\, faculty\, and practitioners across campus ov
 er light refreshments.    </p></li></ul><h3 data-path-to-node="22">  Bio:
  Ambassador Gina Abercrombie-Winstanley</h3><p data-path-to-node="23">  A
 mbassador Gina Abercrombie-Winstanley is a 30-year diplomat\, former U.S.
  Ambassador to the Republic of Malta\, and President of the Middle East P
 olicy Council. Her career spans executive leadership roles across the Dep
 artment of State\, Defense Department\, National Security Council\, and S
 enate Foreign Relations Committee. She has advised the commander of U.S. 
 cyber forces\, expanded international counterterrorism partnerships\, and
  led the emergency evacuation of thousands of U.S. citizens from a war zo
 ne. She is a recipient of the State Department Superior Honor Award for a
 cts of courage during an al-Qaeda attack on the U.S. Consulate in Jeddah.
 </p>
