SUMMARY:Beyond the Name Tag: Making Every Conversation Count at America's
  Role in the World Conference
DESCRIPTION:What Is This Workshop?  Taking place exactly one week before 
 the America's Role in the World (ARW) conference\, this session is an int
 eractive "insider playbook" designed to build your physical muscle memory
  for high-stakes professional networking.  Instead of sitting through a s
 tandard presentation\, you will spend two hours in a hands-on workshop en
 vironment learning how to talk about your background naturally\, receivin
 g real-time coaching\, and gaining the confidence to navigate any room or
  reception with senior leaders\, diplomats\, and industry executives.  Wh
 at You'll Get Out of ItIcebreaking Mechanics &amp\; Follow-Up: Master sim
 ple\, proven frameworks for starting a high-impact conversation\, asking 
 smart questions\, making a memorable impression\, and executing post-conf
 erence follow-ups.    Talk About Your Work Like a Professional: Stop desc
 ribing your classes like homework. Learn how to take standard papers\, re
 search\, or group projects and translate them into 30-second conversation
 al insights that prove you understand real-world policy and global issues
 .    Live Reception Simulation: Participate in a simulated high-stakes ne
 tworking reception. Practice entering active conversation circles with vi
 siting executives\, holding your ground\, and making clean\, professional
  exits.    Real-Time Coaching &amp\; Feedback: Receive immediate\, low-st
 akes feedback on your delivery and conversation flow in small-group lab r
 otations.    Bounce-Back Strategies: Gain actionable tradecraft tactics f
 or handling awkward interactions\, recovering from "networking fails\," a
 nd turning brief encounters into long-term professional connections.     
      Open to all undergraduate and graduate students (Freshmen through Ph
 D candidates) across international affairs\, public policy\, cybersecurit
 y\, data science\, and related disciplines.    Preparation: No prior netw
 orking experience required—just bring an idea of one paper\, class proj
 ect\, or campus activity you've worked on recently!
